## Symptom

On the Askey SBE1V1K (IPQ9574) the CPU never leaves the frequency the bootloader
picked. `cpufreq` is not registered at all and throttling/governor tuning has no
effect:

```
l5: Bringing 0uV into 1800000-1800000uV
l5: failed to enable: -ENXIO
l5: devm_regulator_register() failed, ret=-6
platform cpufreq-dt: deferred probe pending
```

`/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq` does not exist, `/sys/class/regulator/`
contains no `s1`, and `devices_deferred` lists `cpufreq-dt`.

## Root cause

The board DTS declares `mp5496_l5` with `regulator-always-on` / `regulator-boot-on`,
so the regulator core tries to enable it during registration. The RPM firmware on
this board does not expose LDOA5 and answers `-ENXIO`.

`rpm_regulator_probe()` returns on the first failing child node:

```c
for_each_available_child_of_node_scoped(dev->of_node, node) {
        ret = rpm_regulator_init_vreg(vreg, dev, node, vreg_data);
        if (ret < 0)
                return ret;      /* l5 fails here */
}
```

The devm rollback then unregisters `s1` as well. `cpu-supply` loses its provider,
OPP lookup fails and `cpufreq-dt` stays deferred forever, so the CPU stays at the
bootloader frequency.

The `l5` node looks copied from another reference design; the vendor firmware for
this board has no L5 at all.

## Fix

Drop `l5` and describe the rails the vendor firmware actually uses, with the ranges
taken from the dtb dumped out of the `HLOS_1` partition of the stock image:

```dts
&rpm_requests {
	regulators {
		s1 {
			regulator-min-microvolt = <587500>;
			regulator-max-microvolt = <1075000>;
			regulator-always-on;
		};

		ipq9574_s2: s2 {
			regulator-min-microvolt = <700000>;
			regulator-max-microvolt = <1000000>;
			regulator-always-on;
		};
	};
};
```

`s1` is the CPU rail. The lower bound of 587500 gets clamped to 600000 by the
`mp5496_smps` descriptor, which is expected - the 936 MHz OPP already asks for
725 mV, so the floor is never reached in practice.

## Verification

Tested on Askey SBE1V1K, kernel 6.18.55:

| item | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| `scaling_max_freq` | (no cpufreq) | 2208000 |
| available OPPs | - | 936M / 1104M / 1200M / 1416M / 1488M / 1800M / 2208M |
| governor | - | schedutil |
| `s1` regulator | missing | present, enabled |
| `devices_deferred` | `cpufreq-dt` | empty |
| openssl sha256 8K | 392,264 k/s | 1,089,277 k/s (**2.78x**) |
| openssl sha256 16K | 402,889 k/s | 1,124,786 k/s (**2.79x**) |

The speedup matches the 2208/800 ratio, so there is no other bottleneck involved.

Wireless throughput was unchanged (ath12k data path is offloaded), the gain shows up
in NAT, VPN, small-packet forwarding and userspace work.

Background discussion with the same conclusion and the vendor dtb dump:
https://forum.openwrt.org/t/spectrum-sbe1v1k-ipq9574-openwrt-support/245244/242

## Notes

- Only `s1` is required for cpufreq; `s2` is included because it is present in the
  vendor firmware and shares the same probe. If a reviewer prefers the minimal
  change, dropping `l5` alone is enough to unlock the CPU.
- `Supply for s1 (s1) resolved to itself` is a benign warning from the upstream
  supply-name table and is unrelated to this fix.

The packages are published by Digitaliseringsstyrelsen's nuget.org organisation through a Trusted Publishing policy that a member creates. `docs/releasing.md` now says:

- the policy's owner is the organisation, so the packages belong to it;
- `NUGET_USER` is the profile name (not the email address) of the member who created the policy, and changes when another member takes over;
- at least one Digitaliseringsstyrelsen employee should administer the organisation;
- publishing doesn't have to wait for the `DK.Gov.Oio` prefix reservation, which can cover packages already published.

Docs only.

## Outcome and scope

Refs #1158. This PR syncs exactly one accepted change into `release/2026.09-rc1`: #1174, merged into develop as `eb34ba197ee80d7189a6efb54b9eea1f6baaf394`.

The delta from the current RC `c1f092d1` is the commits `186d86a3`, `e38f647b`, `98640ae3` and the merge `eb34ba19`.

**Product fix.** On Windows the launcher now keeps the child's exit code bit-exact. Before, an NTSTATUS ≥ 2^31 was masked as `0xFFFFFFFF` (CPython 3.11 `(int)PyLong_AsLong`).

**Qualification evidence.** The change adds:
- child-wait buckets;
- a closed `observed_exit_class`;
- a gated private faulthandler sink, enabled only for the Windows candidate flow and reduced to a closed `child_fatal_observation`.

Release metadata is unchanged: **2026.09 RC1 (build 261029)**. After the merge, the RC tree equals the develop tree at `eb34ba19`.

**Next step.** Dispatch these on the RC head and record the results in #1158:
- Combined Windows candidate acceptance;
- supervised portable onefile;
- native launcher qualification.

## Gates

#1174 merged on exact head `98640ae3` with:
- fresh required CI;
- Opus 5.5 independent review: CHANGES, then APPROVE;
- Sonnet 5.5 delta review: APPROVE;
- Codex Review: no issues;
- 0 unresolved threads.

This PR adds no new code.

