The Kotlin SDK's protocol types, generated from `specs/src` as the SDK builds. Nothing generated is committed.

- **The generator** is the SDK's own (`src/codegen`), after Rust's. It bundles `specs/src`, knows the shapes the spec uses and refuses any other, so a schema that grows a new shape fails the build.
- **What it writes:** every component schema as a type of `semiont.types` (526 declarations with their inline types), and each kind of id as a value class of `semiont.identifiers`, made only through its rule's check (counted in code points) or `parse`. Decoding goes through the same check.
- **Properties:** a property left out stays left out, and a null stays null. A property that is optional and nullable is a `Stated`: `Absent`, `Null` or `Value`. Dates and URIs stay the text they were. A closed object refuses a member it doesn't name, an open one keeps it (`rest`), and the rest ignore it.
- **Unions** are sealed interfaces their members implement, told apart by their discriminator or by the first member that fits.
- **Java** builds a model through its builder and reaches ids as their own types (`@JvmExposeBoxed`, still experimental in Kotlin 2.4; the README says so).
- **Tests:**
  - every payload of the three conformance corpora read and written back as the JSON it was;
  - the id kinds' accept/refuse cases;
  - a census of the types against the spec;
  - the three states of the one optional nullable member;
  - the generator's refusals;
  - a Java test of builders and ids.
- **Five must-not-compile programs:**
  - text as an id;
  - one kind of id for another;
  - a model changed in place;
  - from Java, text as an id;
  - from Java, a model's private constructor.
- **The ABI dump** holds only hand-written API: the generated types are the spec's, so a spec edit needs no JDK to update the Kotlin dump.
- **CI:** CodeQL now analyses Kotlin, and the Kotlin job fails when it writes no coverage report, so coverage is measured, not just uploaded when present.

The code for each spec table (timing, error codes, telemetry, sign-in, OAuth clients, media types, cache refresh, the bus registry) will come with the phase that first reads it, so nothing generated goes unread.

## Summary

Adds `UserControllerTests`, covering all four endpoints of `UserController`.

## Changes

- `GET /api/v1/users`:
  - default pagination (page size 20, `id` ascending), empty page, filters combined with pagination and sorting, and the `active=false` filter;
  - page size capped at 100 and each whitelisted sort property accepted;
  - sort properties outside the whitelist rejected with 400, including when only one of several is invalid;
  - invalid `role`/`active` parameters (400) and unexpected service failure (500).
- `POST /api/v1/users`:
  - successful creation, with a check that `password` is not returned, and every valid role;
  - 19 validation scenarios, each with a single violation: username (required, length, format, reserved name in any case), email (required, empty, format, length), password (required, length, 72-byte limit, strength rules) and role;
  - missing body and invalid role value in the JSON (400);
  - 409 for duplicate username and duplicate email, and 500.
- `PATCH /api/v1/users/{userId}/reactivate`: success, invalid ID (400), user not found (404), system user (409) and 500.
- `PATCH /api/v1/users/{userId}/deactivate`: success, invalid ID (400), self-deactivation (403), user not found (404), last active administrator (409) and 500.

## Notes

- Uses the same structure as `ClientControllerTests`: `@WebMvcTest` with `WebMvcTestSecurityConfig` and `PaginationConfig`, a mocked `UserService`, `@Nested` per endpoint and `@MethodSource` for invalid request bodies.
- `@PreAuthorize` is not exercised, since filters are disabled in this slice, as in the other controller tests.
- No production code changes.
